AWS CloudWatch in Plain English ☁️

Launching AWS resources is easy.
Knowing what’s happening inside them is the real skill. That’s where CloudWatch comes in.

♦️What CloudWatch actually does? 🤔

CloudWatch helps you monitor:

• CPU usage
• Memory patterns
• Logs
• Errors
• Alarms
• Application health

Basically:
“What is my system doing right now?”

♦️Why it matters? 🤔

Without CloudWatch:

❌ You don’t know why your app failed
❌ You miss traffic spikes
❌ You find issues too late
❌ Debugging becomes guesswork

With CloudWatch:

✅ Logs are visible
✅ Metrics are tracked
✅ Alerts notify you early
✅ Systems become easier to troubleshoot

♦️Real example:

If Your EC2 app slows down.

CloudWatch can show:

• CPU is 95%
• Error logs increased
• Traffic spiked
• Alarm was triggered

Now you know what happened.

♦️Where it’s used? 🤔

• EC2 monitoring
• Lambda logs
• API Gateway errors
• RDS performance
• Billing alarms
• Production alerts

🚨Beginner mistakes:

❌ No alarms
❌ Never checking logs
❌ Monitoring only after something breaks
❌ Not setting billing alerts

💡Golden rule:
If you deploy it, monitor it.
